37:- a scorecard, rubric, or benchmark memo is needed
38:- an attached guide, canon, or pattern document is being used to benchmark prompt-stack reflection or chapter-family carryover
46:- the work is exploratory and not evaluation-driven
50:Produce trustworthy evaluation and monitoring signals that improve deployment and versioning decisions.
53:- choosing the right evaluation surface
60:## 3. Evaluation Surfaces
83:## 4. Contract-First Evaluation
96:- evaluate against what was actually promised
107:- trajectory artifacts should exist if process quality is being evaluated
177:- compare on the same evaluation surface
191:- when evaluating agentic tool behavior, prefer mock tools or a dedicated safe test environment over live irreversible systems
194:### 7.1 Preferred evaluation packets
196:When evaluation work must be reused or reviewed, prefer compact artifacts such as:
197:- mock-tool evaluation report
198:- safe trajectory artifact report
199:- benchmark comparison memo
201:- guide reflection benchmark memo when chapter-family question / answer / verdict loops are the live review surface
203:- packet compliance report when required-vs-optional artifact coverage is itself under review
205:- operational substrate readiness memo
212:- HITL approval packet
213:- benchmark registry memo when scenario identity, cohort scope, or replay support must be defined before comparison
215:- critique quality review memo when iteration quality or no-gain-loop behavior is itself under evaluation
218:- coding benchmark scenario memo when coding-agent proof depends on repo scope and verification-running expectations
219:- benchmark execution report when compared candidates have actually been run
220:- replay suite verdict memo when replay execution state and verdict reproducibility matter
221:- context failure taxonomy memo when substrate diagnosis is itself the evaluation output
222:- critique utility scorecard when refinement value, not mere critique presence, is the question
228:- plan approval checkpoint artifact
230:Packet rule:
231:- preserve the smallest artifact that lets another reviewer reproduce the gate decision
263:- if lifecycle labeling, checkpoint artifacts, or candidate identity are weak, narrow the gate conclusion before escalating it
274:- packet-family completeness regression
275:- control-loop packet parity regression
277:- skill-layer packet parity regression
282:- packet-compliance regression
283:- behavior-replay coverage regression
308:- control-loop packet parity gate
310:- skill-layer packet parity gate
313:- benchmark-registry gate
315:- critique-quality gate
318:- benchmark-execution gate
319:- replay-verdict gate
323:- packet-compliance gate
324:- behavior-replay gate
362:### 10.3 Guide-reflection benchmark loop
366:- generate benchmark questions that test decision-quality answerability, not mere keyword presence
369:- patch the highest-signal gaps first, then rerun the same benchmark set instead of silently swapping questions
380:- document-benchmark to assembled-replay escalation
384:- for `runtime owner integrity`, document-level benchmark is sufficient when the reviewed runtime surfaces directly expose the owner boundary and no required behavior depends only on operator-facing prose
385:- for `benchmark-loop adequacy`, document-level benchmark is sufficient when the reviewed runtime surfaces directly expose the question -> answer -> verification -> rerun loop and explicit stop rule
386:- escalate those families to replay only when the claim is no longer about document/runtime parity and now depends on assembled behavior or actual runner execution state
388:Packet preference:
389:- `Benchmark registry memo` for chapter-family inventory and expected verdict surface
390:- `Guide reflection benchmark memo` for question / answer / comparison / rerun tracking
392:- `Packet compliance report` when packet-floor doctrine is part of the failure surface
394:Operational escalation rule:
395:- if the live question has shifted from document reflection to assembled prompt behavior, escalate from `Guide reflection benchmark memo` to `Benchmark cohort manifest`
396:- if replay has actually been attempted, preserve `Replay runner verdict sheet` rather than prose-only replay status
397:- if multiple replayed scenarios must be summarized into one decision, preserve `Replay suite verdict memo` and keep partial coverage explicit
401:Use judge-like qualitative evaluation only when:
420:- restate the evaluation scope and compared systems
423:- define the evaluation surface, criteria, and relevant thresholds
433:- state what evidence the evaluation is based on
437:### 12.1 Evaluation close-out rule
439:A good Codex-facing eval close-out usually keeps:
454:- evaluation theater with no decision model
460:Evaluate only what matters for the decision.
467:Prefer linked operational artifacts such as `Benchmark cohort manifest`, `Replay runner verdict sheet`, `Release promotion decision record`, and `Telemetry drift investigation memo` when the gate depends on repeated execution evidence.
468:Keep `scenario_id`, `run_id`, `cohort_id`, `trace_id`, and `artifact_version` stable across benchmark, replay, release, and telemetry gates.
469:If the required packet floor is missing or joined artifacts fail precedence, compatibility, freshness, or completeness checks, downgrade the gate and keep `false-promotion`, `false-hold`, and `drift-triggered review` separate.
